      "problem": "$-\\frac{7}{4}\\,(-h)$ is equivalent to",
      "worked_solution": "First, remember that the product of two negative numbers is a positive number. Thus, \n\n$$\n-\\frac{7}{4}(-h)=\\frac{7}{4}h.\n$$\n\nNow, writing $h$ as $\\frac{h}{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{4}h & =\\frac{7}{4}\\cdot h \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}\\cdot \\frac{h}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\cdot h}{4\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{7h}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the expressions $-\\frac{7}{4}(-h)$ and $\\frac{7h}{4}$ are equivalent.",
